# M-readable-bytes, panel 163. **There was no second wall**, and this case is the
# proof the sitting could not find in its own brief.
#
# THE QUESTION WAS: how does a program obtain a header record with a long array
# field, so it can hand it to a C function that FILLS it? The brief said the only
# route was a literal with one element per slot — 803 characters for a 256-field,
# five times over for `struct utsname`. Three seats ran the thing the brief had
# not: **ask for one**. A function that returns the record is all it takes, and
# `partial` lets the program declare only the fields it reads.
#
# **THIS CASE USES NO C THE AUTHOR WROTE**, and that is the whole point. The
# completeness critic separated two variants nobody else had: one with a
# `static inline` shim in the author's own header, and one with a HEROES function
# returning the record. The second is the route the resolution names, because
# `.claude/rules/c-boundary.md` says the FFI must be complete — *a library Heroes
# cannot bind is a library the author must leave C code around for* — and an
# answer that required a shim would be adopting the thing that rule calls a
# failure. Priced on `heroes measure`: the full literal is 3979 tokens, the Heroes
# producer 874, a shim 159. The shim is an optimisation, never the route.
#
# WHAT WAS REFUSED, so nobody proposes it again as new. A call typed by context
# (`repeat(0, 256)`): C cannot return an array, so the call must be erased at
# compile time and this compiler has no constant folding — measured, the grep
# returns nothing. A zero default: 120 to 180 lines across six modules, and
# design.md §4.9 says no default values. An out-parameter needing no initial
# value: **Rust shipped that and withdrew it in writing**, and this project's own
# `selfhost/ir/inout.hero` already records a deleted out-cell store printing the
# same four lines at -O0 AND under both sanitisers, because the indeterminate
# local happened to be zero.
#
# The field is short here because the length is not what the case is about; the
# mechanism is identical at 256 and the run below proves the shape, not the size.

extern "ffi-a-filled-record-is-asked-for.h"
    record Slot tag slot partial
        name: i8[8]
    function slot_fill(@s: Slot) -> i32

# The producer, in HEROES, with no C behind it. A record a C function fills is
# obtained rather than built, and this is what obtaining one looks like when the
# author writes no C at all.
function blank() -> Slot
    return Slot(name: [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0])

function main()
    s: Slot @ blank()
    _ = slot_fill(@s).to_i64().must()
    print(s.name.validated_bytes().must())
